From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 18:21:12 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: retire dead values before MSL translation

MSL type inference reaches a value only through its uses, so a value whose
last consumer disappears keeps no type at all. The final copy propagation in
msl_optimize_nir does exactly that: propagating a copy through a widened
vector abandons the vector and its mov, and nir_to_msl then translates those
survivors, emitting a literal "UNTYPED!" for every constant they read. Metal
rejects the resulting source, the pipeline never compiles, and the draw
silently contributes nothing. A fragment shader that writes a constant ivec3
to a four-component integer render target hits this, so the OpenGL CTS reads
back uninitialized attachment memory:
dEQP-GL45-ES31.functional.shaders.builtin_constants.core.max_compute_work_group_count
and .max_compute_work_group_size report garbage for the vertex, fragment and
geometry stages while the buffer-backed tessellation and compute stages, which
never build the wide vector, report the correct values.

Run dead code elimination after that propagation, as the Intel and Imagination
backends do after their own out-of-SSA copy propagation, so no untyped value
reaches translation. Add a regression that draws single-pixel points into a
R32G32B32A32_SINT attachment from both a constant fragment output and a flat
integer varying, covering the one-pixel target the shader executor uses.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit af95235f0e96aeb17acd7fa489805c110ee12fc7)
(cherry picked from commit 70d4f0c9fccbe129c1873254a9d05e4bb878269a)
